{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Bounded self-cleaning live-Jira-Cloud coordinator lifecycle lane + search-index-lag hardening\n\n## Problem / root cause\nThe epic's Live-External AC requires the RP-03 coordinator's create / binding-lifecycle /\ncommit-unknown recovery / fuse exercised end-to-end against **live Jira Cloud**. Today the only Cloud\nlane (`jira-cloud-s3-rehearsal`) is read-only AND gated off (`vars.AWS_S3_REHEARSAL_ROLE_ARN` unset) — it\nis SKIPPED even in fully-green scheduled runs, so **no run has ever exercised live-Cloud mutation**.\nSeparately, `rebar bridge check-access` against live REB proves CREATE/LABEL/PROPERTY/DELETE work but its\n`STEP_JQL_SEARCH` flakes because Jira Cloud's Lucene index is eventually consistent and the probe's wait is\na FIXED `JQL_RETRY_COUNT=6` x `JQL_RETRY_SLEEP=5s` (no backoff), shorter than observed Cloud index lag. The\nwrite always lands (the by-key PROPERTY_READ immediately after passes); only SEARCH visibility lags.\n\n## Scope of this leaf\n\n### §1 — Harden the search-index-visibility wait (`access_check.py`)\nReplace the fixed 6x5s loop with an env-tunable EXPONENTIAL-BACKOFF budget:\n- `JIRA_PROBE_JQL_RETRIES` (default **10**; was 6)\n- `JIRA_PROBE_JQL_SLEEP` base seconds (default **3**; was fixed 5)\n- `JIRA_PROBE_JQL_SLEEP_MAX` per-attempt cap seconds (default **30**)\nSchedule: the sleep before retry k (k over 0..RETRIES-2) is `min(base * 2**k, cap)`; with defaults that is\n`[3,6,12,24,30,30,30,30,30]` = **195s worst-case total**, covering the observed Cloud lag (probe timed out\nat the old ~25s budget) with wide margin. The `sleep_fn` injectable seam is preserved so the schedule is\nunit-testable without real sleeping. This helper is reused by §2's commit-unknown visibility wait.\n\n### §2 — Live-Cloud coordinator lifecycle test module (bounded, self-cleaning)\nNEW external module `tests/external/live_jira_cloud_mutation/test_cloud_coordinator_lifecycle.py`\n(+ `conftest.py` for readiness/marker plumbing). Against live REB Cloud it exercises the epic's OWN\ncutover glue **`rebar_reconciler.batch_dispatch.coordinate_and_fuse`** (batch_dispatch.py:314) directly,\nfor a plan list of EXACTLY ONE throwaway ticket:\n\n- **Structural write-bounding (addresses the shared-prod safety concern).** The module does NOT run a\n  full `bridge sync` and does NOT use the legacy-route `--filter-local-ids` flag (which\n  `request.py:95` rejects on the coordinator/primary route anyway). Instead it constructs a\n  `ticket_plans` list of ONE throwaway plan and calls `coordinate_and_fuse([plan], execute=...)`. Because\n  the coordinator only ever acts on the plans it is HANDED (create_coordinator.py `coordinate_create`\n  issues exactly ONE create per plan), and the module hands it exactly one, there is NO project\n  enumeration and NO inbound write-back path — the ONLY Jira mutations physically possible are the single\n  throwaway issue's create + its binding metadata + the final delete. This is a STRONGER guarantee than the\n  DC harness's scrubbed-copy store (which still enumerates ~2700 tickets); here the plan set is one.\n  As a belt-and-suspenders check the module asserts the returned `CutoverReport` tally shows exactly one\n  create outcome and its bound key equals the throwaway key (no other key appears in `report.outcomes`).\n- **create + binding-lifecycle.** The `execute` callback issues the physical create via the live Cloud\n  bridge client and returns the new key; the module asserts the coordinator bound the throwaway local id\n  to that key (`BindingLifecycle`/`bind_confirm`), with `jira` channel + `jira-` local-id provenance.\n- **commit-unknown recovery.** A second invocation injects an AMBIGUOUS create signal (simulated\n  timeout/connection-loss) so `_resolve_ambiguous` (create_coordinator.py:214) re-observes EXACTLY ONCE\n  via the label search and ADOPTS the already-created issue (recovered/commit_unknown) rather than issuing\n  a duplicate. The `observe` callback FIRST waits until the dedup label search\n  (`labels = \"rebar-id:<uuid>\"`) reflects the just-created issue — reusing §1's exponential-backoff\n  visibility helper — so the adoption is attributable to the coordinator's dedup path and cannot flake or\n  duplicate on index lag.\n- **fuse.** `coordinate_and_fuse` folds the outcomes through `PassFuse`; the module asserts the returned\n  `CutoverReport` carries the fuse tally/decisions for the throwaway identity (the fuse path executed).\n- **teardown (always).** The created Cloud issue is deleted by key in a `finally` (mirroring\n  access_check.py:217-224). A label-sweep safety net then lists+deletes any issue carrying THIS run's\n  unique `rebar-id:<uuid>` label (the run's label ONLY, never the shared `rebar-id:` prefix), so a\n  mid-test crash cannot leak.\n- Gated by a module-level `_live_jira_ready()` sentinel + `REBAR_RUN_EXTERNAL=1` + Cloud creds\n  (`JIRA_URL/JIRA_USER/JIRA_API_TOKEN/JIRA_PROJECT`), enrolling in the all-skip canary\n  (`tests/external/conftest.py`) exactly like the DC modules.\n\n### §3 — Workflow lane\nAdd `jira-cloud-mutation-probe` to `.github/workflows/external-integration.yml` (`workflow_dispatch` +\nweekly cron, like the sibling lanes): sets the REB Cloud env from repo secrets, runs `rebar bridge\ncheck-access` (capability preflight) THEN the new live-Cloud lifecycle module, and on BOTH failure AND\ncancel runs an always-run teardown step that sweeps the run's uniquely-labeled probe issues via acli —\nlisting with `acli jira workitem search --jql 'labels = \"rebar-id:<run-unique>\"' --json` and deleting each\nhit with `acli jira workitem delete --key <KEY> --yes` (the `--yes` is REQUIRED per the acli.py bug-3256\nnote so the non-TTY CI sweep does not block on stdin and leak issues). The `<run-unique>` label is composed\nfrom the GitHub run id so the sweep is scoped to the CURRENT run only, never the shared prefix.\n\n### §4 — Workflow-structure unit test\n`tests/unit/test_external_integration_cloud_mutation_workflow.py` (modelled on\n`tests/unit/test_jira_dc_capability_map_workflow.py`) proves the new lane exists, runs the check-access\npreflight + the lifecycle module, and has the always-run teardown sweep step with `--yes` scoped to the\nrun-unique label.\n\n### §5 — Docs\n`tests/external/live_jira_cloud_s3/README.md` updated to document that the read-only cardinal rule is that\nsuite's design choice, and that the NEW mutating lane is bounded (one throwaway plan), self-cleaning, and\nsafe for shared prod.\n\n## Consumers to update (enumerated)\n- `tests/scripts/bridge/test_jira_capability_probe.py` pins the CURRENT fixed budget: it asserts\n  `search_issues.call_count == 6` and inter-attempt `delays == [5,5,5,5,5]`. This change makes the default\n  10 attempts with backoff, so this test is updated in the SAME change to assert the new default attempt\n  count and the new exponential-backoff `delays == [3,6,12,24,30,30,30,30,30]` (driven off the same\n  constants/env defaults, not magic numbers), plus an env-override case that recomputes the schedule from\n  an overridden base/cap/retries — so it stays a real oracle rather than a change-detector.\n\n## Surface-parity census\n- **CLI** (`rebar bridge check-access`): in scope — the JQL backoff is exercised through it.\n- **library** (`access_check.run_access_check`; `batch_dispatch.coordinate_and_fuse`): in scope — unit\n  tests drive the backoff schedule directly; the live module drives the coordinator glue directly.\n- **automated caller** (the new workflow lane / live module): in scope — the E2E oracle is the dispatched\n  live run.\n- **MCP/API**: out of scope — neither `run_access_check` nor `coordinate_and_fuse` is an MCP tool.\n- **dual-direction**: the coordinator's outbound create is exercised; there is no inbound write-back in a\n  one-plan invocation (structurally — see §2), so there is no mirror direction to cover here.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] `access_check.py` JQL wait is env-tunable exponential backoff; defaults `[3,6,12,24,30,...]` capped at\n  30, 10 attempts; `sleep_fn` seam preserved; write-landing still confirmed by the by-key read; the helper\n  is reused by §2's visibility wait.\n- [ ] `tests/scripts/bridge/test_jira_capability_probe.py` updated to the new schedule/defaults and passes.\n- [ ] New live-Cloud lifecycle module drives `coordinate_and_fuse` for ONE throwaway plan, exercises\n  create/binding-lifecycle + idempotent-adoption commit-unknown (after a dedup-label visibility wait) +\n  fuse, asserts the tally touches only the throwaway key, and ALWAYS deletes (finally + run-label sweep).\n  Enrols in the all-skip canary.\n- [ ] New `jira-cloud-mutation-probe` workflow lane runs check-access + the lifecycle module with an\n  always-run run-label teardown sweep using `acli ... delete --key <KEY> --yes`; proven by the\n  workflow-structure unit test.\n- [ ] A dispatched Live External Integration run shows the new Cloud-mutation lane GREEN and the DC lane\n  GREEN, satisfying the epic's (amended) Live-External AC.\n- [ ] `make lint && make typecheck && make test` green.\n\n## Verify commands\n- `make test PYTEST_ARGS=\"tests/scripts/bridge/test_jira_capability_probe.py tests/unit/test_external_integration_cloud_mutation_workflow.py -q\"`\n- `make lint`\n\n## Portability\nThe hardening and the lifecycle test are transport/CI-agnostic: the backoff is a library behavior (no CI\ntrigger), and the new workflow lane surfaces an operation-linked probe (`rebar bridge check-access` + a\npytest module driving `coordinate_and_fuse`) that also runs locally with the same env — no capability is\ngated solely on a specific CI system.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787763820198135001,"uuid":"6be5278b-0c54-494d-a4a5-1ac3606d3b2d"}